<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n22890" root="شكو and شكى" body-key="$">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَاكٍ</span></b> : see <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَكِىٌّ</span>, in two places. ＝   
    <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer1"> </span>-A2-  In the phrase
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">رَجُلٌ شَاكِى السِّلَاحِ</span>, (S,) which means <i>A man
whose weapon is sharp,</i> or <i>whose weapons are
sharp,</i> (S, K, *) Akh says that <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شاكى</span> is formed
by transposition from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَائِك</span> [q. v. in art. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شوك</span>]:
(S:) and accord. to AZ, one says also <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَاكٍ فِى</span>
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">السِّلاحِ</span>. (TA in art. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شوك</span>.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  And <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الشَّاكِى</span> [is
app. formed in like manner from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الشَّائِكُ</span>, and]
signifies <i>The lion.</i> (K.)

